course: dictionary
lesson: 3450
title: Factors: numbers that divide exactly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A factor is a whole number that divides exactly into another number, with no remainder. 3 is a factor of 12, because 12 ÷ 3 = 4 exactly. Factors come in pairs that multiply to make the number: 3 × 4 = 12.
explain: The word also means one of the things that causes or affects a result. «Two-factor authentication asks for two things, your password and a code sent to your phone, so a stolen password alone is not enough to get in.» Each thing is a factor. Check with the swap test: if you put «thing» in place of «factor» and the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: To factor something in means to include it when you plan or work something out. «Two-factor authentication means you need the password and something else, like a code on your phone.» The words around factor give you the clue: the password and the code are both needed, so both are counted.
fact: A factor is a whole number that divides exactly into another number.
fact: To find the factors of 12, look for pairs that multiply to 12: 1 × 12, 2 × 6, 3 × 4.
fact: A factor can also be one of the things that affects a result, like the weather affecting when you go out.
fact: A number such as «factor 50» on sun cream shows how strong the protection is.
exercise: Which number is a factor of 12? | 5 | 7 | 8 | *4
exercise: «Two-factor authentication asks for two things, your password and a code sent to your phone.» What does factor mean here? | A type of phone | A whole number that divides exactly | *One of the things that is needed to get in | A mistake in the password
exercise: Mum says, «The weather is a factor in when we go to the park.» What does factor mean? | A number that divides exactly into another | *One thing that affects the decision | A sum of money | A rule about bags
test: Which of these is NOT a factor of 6? | 1 | 2 | 3 | *4
test: «Sam factored in the rain when he planned his day.» What does factored in mean? | He ignored the rain completely | *He included the rain when he planned | He counted the raindrops | He bought a new umbrella for the trip
exam: «Two-factor authentication means you need the password and something else.» How many things do you need to get in? | Three | None | One | *Two
